<body>
<h1 style="text-align: center;"><font size="5">A general education in mathematics -&nbsp;</font><span style="font-size: x-large;">the middle way</span></h1><p><font size="5">The web site <a href="https://www.middlewaymath.app">middlewaymath.app</a> describes curriculum and content for a general education in mathematics. This site is maintained by a hobby level project, which is under constant modification. Yet the page must be considered "unvetted" information as the "hobbyist" has neither bona fides nor actual expertise, only opinions.</font></p><p><font size="5">A general education targets all students. It's explicit intent is not to prepare students for further study in mathematics; rather it's purpose is to provide an overview of the role of mathematics in mathematical physics and the modern world.&nbsp;</font><span style="font-size: x-large;">In fact, one expects that a general education will not provide the practical knowledge some students will require even for further mathematical studies. If we, without much precision, categorize knowledge as practical, formal, or conceptual, then we may describe the curricular target is to move the needle from conceptual to formal.</span></p><p><font size="5">Mathematics is assumed to be a formal science. This assumption provides the page's perspective on the ontological status of mathematics: it lives in a world of supposition, a bridge that connects our intuition with our experience, a world where we ask "what if?", and see where it leads, and, eventually, how well it can fit.</font></p><p><font size="5">However, this ontological stance does not preclude the possibility that the formalism models something. In fact, this is assumed by the page. It allows middle way mathematics to be described as an effective model for a pedagogic description of modern mathematics.</font></p><p><font size="5">The hallmark of any formal science is that it is expressed formally, not naturally, although the formality will be embedded in explanation. The formality a mathematical community adopts will impact their school of thought. The page assumes an unadorned single-sorted FOL and ZF set theory as it's formal foundation. That is, the page adopts about the simplest formal foundation possible.&nbsp;</font><span style="font-size: x-large;">But it important to note that the page makes no claim that this formalism has the expressive power to model all of mathematics.&nbsp;</span></p><p><font size="5">An effective model of mathematics takes as an axiomatic foundation an established mathematical fact. In the case of middle way mathematics, this axiomatic foundation is the mathematical fact, fully established in 2001, that</font></p><p></p><ul><li><font size="5"><b>(No</b>(ε<sub>0</sub>), +,&nbsp; ·) is an algebraic field.</font></li></ul><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">To describe the axiom, we type ε</span><sub>0</sub><span style="font-size: x-large;"> as an ordinal. An ordinal is a primitive type. Here too, the model becomes effective, as ordinals have been modeled by using von Neumann sets as class partition instances of (set, well-order) pairs. We merely assume the existence of a well ordered spline the includes the naturals as starting this spline, which also includes the supremum of ℕ, i.e. ω, and ε</span><sub>0</sub><span style="font-size: x-large;">.</span></div><p></p><div><font size="5"><b>No</b> is the class of nodes of a tree defined by 2-successor transfinite induction;&nbsp;</font><b style="font-size: x-large;">No</b><span style="font-size: x-large;"> can be constructed in various ways. The page uses the ordinals for birthday values. Either implicitly or explicitly, each tree node, i.e. number, has a birthday.</span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">One can consider all tree nodes whose birthday is less than some cutoff ordinal α; this is denoted <b>No</b>(α). The field level operations +, - are defined by transfinite recursive definitions originally described by Conway, and No will sometimes be referred to as the Conway number tree.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">What the foundational axiom claims is that there is an ordinal ε<sub>0</sub>, greater than ω, which is the first ordinal which, when used as a cutoff, identifies an algebraic field, the ambient field for arithmetic. We do not use this field directly; ε<sub>0</sub> may be unimaginably small by ordinal standards, but from a computational perspective, it is unimaginably large. However, we are now assured that any subset of <b>No</b>(ε<sub>0</sub>) will inherit field level arithmetic operations.</span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;"><br></span></div><div><font size="5">Middle way mathematics identifies two important subsets of <b>No</b>(ε<sub>0</sub>), namely <b>No</b>(ω) and <b>No</b>(ω+1).</font></div><div><font size="5"><br></font></div><div><font size="5">As ω is the first non-finite ordinal, <b>No</b>(ω) identifies the set of numbers with finite birthdays. Two important facts are established.&nbsp;</font></div><div><ul><li><font size="5">the recursive arithmetic definitions that hold for the whole tree, via transfinite induction, are describe by finite induction for numbers whose birthdays are finite; and</font></li><li><font size="5">and (</font><b style="font-size: x-large;">No</b><span style="font-size: x-large;">(ω), +, ·) is isomorphic to (𝔻, +, ·), where operations for No(ω) are Conway's recursively defined operations, and&nbsp;</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">(𝔻, +, ·) denotes standard dyadic arithmetic.&nbsp;</span></li></ul><div><font size="5">In middle way mathematics, all numerical calculation occurs within the realm of the ring of dyadic arithmetic.</font></div><div><font size="5"><br></font></div><div><font size="5">For middle way mathematics, a (non-complex) number is a member of <b>No</b>(ω+1), where page notation assumes ℝ_ω ≡&nbsp;</font><span style="font-size: x-large;"><b>No</b>(ω+1). The notation ℝ_ω suggests that, in middle way mathematics, ℝ_ω plays the role of the real numbers in standard mathematical curricula. But ℝ_ω is not the real numbers. It is often said that ℝ ⊂&nbsp;</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">ℝ_ω; but no such claim is made: ℝ allows a grid size of zero, which is made sense of only by a theory of limits, a theory of continuity in an Archimedean field. ℝ_ω is what it is: nodes on the Conway number tree whose birthday is less or equal to ω.</span></div></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">An interesting consequence is that, for middle way mathematics, the ontological status of 1/3 and 1/ω are equivalent. Both are numbers whose birthdays is ω. Another thing to note is that</span></div><div><ul><li><span style="font-size: x-large;">(ℝ_ω, +, ·) is not a field, as it is not closed under arithmetic operations.</span></li></ul><div><font size="5">However, the result of such operations will be a member of <b>No</b>(ε<sub>0</sub>) and so will be a perfectly valid Conway tree number; it just happens the result is not in&nbsp;</font><span style="font-size: x-large;">ℝ_ω. In this sense, we indirectly deal with numbers in&nbsp;</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">&nbsp;</span><b style="font-size: x-large;">No</b><span style="font-size: x-large;">(ε</span><span style="font-size: 0.8em; vertical-align: -0.25em; line-height: 0;">0</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">): we express mathematical statements using numbers in</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">&nbsp;</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">ℝ_ω.</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">&nbsp;An actual number might not lie in ℝ_ω, but it certainly a member of <b>No</b>(ε<sub>0</sub>).&nbsp;</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">But since ℝ_ω is not used for numerical calculation - we stated above all calculation is wrt the ring (𝔻, +, ·) - the fact that ℝ_ω is not closed wrt these operations is not particularly relevant. What is highly relevant is, when mathematical structure is described using ℝ_ω, the description can include infinitesimal values with as much confidence as using π or 1/3.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">To summarize</span></div><div><ul><li><span style="font-size: x-large;">this allows limit theory to be replaced by algebraic manipulation</span></li><li><span style="font-size: x-large;">and where a</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">&nbsp;standard part function is established that defines a unique dyadic rational for any number in </span><b style="font-size: x-large;">No</b><span style="font-size: x-large;">(ε</span><span style="font-size: 0.8em; vertical-align: -0.25em; line-height: 0;">0</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">).</span></li></ul></div></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">This approach allows for a clean separation of algebraic manipulation and numeric calculation.</span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">Middle way mathematics uses Lean 4 to verify it's formal statements. The project includes two source code files&nbsp;</span></div><div><ul><li><span style="font-size: x-large;">&nbsp;one for formal statements describing the axioms, definitions and theorems of middle way mathematics</span></li><li><span style="font-size: x-large;">the other for formal statements underlying numeric calculation.</span></li></ul><div><font size="5">The first file listed contains noncomputable source code; the second file contains computable source code which implements dyadic arithmetic.&nbsp;</font></div><div><font size="5">The first file contains axioms, definitions and theorems corresponding to the fundamental axioms, definitions and theorems of middle way mathematics. For example this is where the primitive type ordinal and the fundamental axiom that&nbsp;</font><b style="font-size: x-large;">No</b><span style="font-size: x-large;">(ε</span><span style="font-size: 0.8em; vertical-align: -0.25em; line-height: 0;">0</span><span style="font-size: x-large;">) is an algebraic field are described.</span></div><div><span style="font-size: x-large;">The second file implements dyadic arithmetic in a manner that exactly matches the formal description of&nbsp; middle way mathematics: the set is defined by finite induction, while order and arithmetic operations are defined recursively.</span></div></div><div><font size="5"><br></font></div><div><font size="5">The current middle way mathematics application web site hosts a static page. There is currently no runtime connection to Lean. Instead the project includes a development environment that includes Node based utilities that use a local installation of Lean to compile and cache information for the application. For this reason the application cannot currently demonstrate Lean calculation of dyadic arithmetic. This is a minor inconvenience because not only would using a recursive based definitions be extremely impractical, an important theorem establishes an isomorphism between recursive definitions for finite numbers and the rules for dyadic arithmetic. Nevertheless, the application includes rules to implement the recursive definitions, mainly to demonstrate their equivalence with dyadic arithmetic, and their calculational inefficiency.</font></div><div><font size="5"><br></font></div><div><font size="5">The more important arithmetic engine built into the application is a dyadic arithmetic machine that operates on the nodes of the Conway number tree whose birthdays are finite. This is the engine used by the application's rule interpreter, where a rule is a block of pseudocode responsible for assigning a member of the directed pair's codomain to any member of the directed pair's domain of the directed pair of a directed equality. The following diagram illustrates middle way mathematics terminology.</font></div><div><div align="center" style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: &quot;Times New Roman&quot;; margin: 16px 0px;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" data-diagram-name="ontologyModel" style="background: transparent; color-scheme: light dark; cursor: pointer;" version="1.1" width="580px" height="532px" viewBox="0 0 580 532" id="ge-svg-x2b6Yz-zumwQZEyoxsQR" content="&lt;mxfile host=&quot;embed.diagrams.net&quot; agent=&quot;Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT         10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko)         draw.io/29.3.6 Chrome/140.0.7339.249 Electron/38.8.0         Safari/537.36&quot;&gt;&lt;diagram name=&quot;Page-1&quot; id=&quot;5oqYsMtTyfGbQJYCZnEy&quot;&gt;&lt;/diagram&gt;&lt;/mxfile&gt;" title="Double-click to edit diagram in Draw.io"><style type="text/css">@supports (color: light-dark(#000, #fff)) { #ge-svg-x2b6Yz-zumwQZEyoxsQR { --ge-adaptive-bg: light-dark(#ffffff, var(--ge-dark-color, #121212)); } }</style><defs></defs><g><g data-cell-id="0"><g data-cell-id="1"><g data-cell-id="ZIRW3RkSEAOvqEjLKah4-38"><g></g><g data-cell-id="tkclq1CocFOdeJo-cvJn-10"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><path d="M 183.68 168.11 L 183.7 131.9 L 160.5 131.9 L 160.46 101.37" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="stroke" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 180.18 162.22 L 183.68 169.23 L 187.18 162.23" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 160.45 94.37 L 162.52 97.86 L 160.46 101.37 L 158.4 97.87 Z" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="fill: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255)); stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path></g></g><g data-cell-id="8fY0ih4KfIxNHs2CR79k-1"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><ellipse cx="183.68" cy="186.79" rx="55.92" ry="16.44" fill="none" stroke="#000000" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></ellipse></g><g><g><switch><foreignObject style="overflow: visible; text-align: left;" pointer-events="none" width="100%" height="100%" requiredFeatures="http://www.w3.org/TR/SVG11/feature#Extensibility"><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="display: flex; align-items: unsafe center; justify-content: unsafe center; width: 110px; height: 1px; padding-top: 187px; margin-left: 129px;"><div style="box-sizing: border-box; font-size: 0; text-align: center; color: #000000; "><div style="display: inline-block; font-size: 12px; font-family: Helvetica; color: light-dark(#000000, #ffffff); line-height: 1.2; pointer-events: all; white-space: normal; word-wrap: normal; ">proposition</div></div></div></foreignObject><text x="184" y="190" fill="#000000" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="12px" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));">proposition</text></switch></g></g></g><g data-cell-id="tkclq1CocFOdeJo-cvJn-11"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><path d="M 160.46 276.46 L 160.5 241 L 183.7 241 L 183.68 211.21" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="stroke" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 156.97 270.57 L 160.46 277.58 L 163.97 270.58" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 183.68 204.21 L 185.74 207.71 L 183.68 211.21 L 181.62 207.72 Z" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="fill: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255)); stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path></g></g><g data-cell-id="8fY0ih4KfIxNHs2CR79k-2"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><ellipse cx="160.46" cy="295.14" rx="55.92" ry="16.44" fill="none" stroke="#000000" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></ellipse></g><g><g><switch><foreignObject style="overflow: visible; text-align: left;" pointer-events="none" width="100%" height="100%" requiredFeatures="http://www.w3.org/TR/SVG11/feature#Extensibility"><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="display: flex; align-items: unsafe center; justify-content: unsafe center; width: 110px; height: 1px; padding-top: 295px; margin-left: 106px;"><div style="box-sizing: border-box; font-size: 0; text-align: center; color: #000000; "><div style="display: inline-block; font-size: 12px; font-family: Helvetica; color: light-dark(#000000, #ffffff); line-height: 1.2; pointer-events: all; white-space: normal; word-wrap: normal; ">quantifier</div></div></div></foreignObject><text x="160" y="299" fill="#000000" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="12px" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));">quantifier</text></switch></g></g></g><g data-cell-id="tkclq1CocFOdeJo-cvJn-14"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><path d="M 419.53 311.58 L 419.53 358.39" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="stroke" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 419.53 365.39 L 416.03 358.39 L 423.03 358.39 Z" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path></g></g><g data-cell-id="tkclq1CocFOdeJo-cvJn-16"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><path d="M 419.53 276.46 L 419.5 241 L 183.7 241 L 183.68 211.21" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="stroke" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 416.03 270.58 L 419.53 277.58 L 423.03 270.57" fill="none" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path><path d="M 183.68 204.21 L 185.74 207.71 L 183.68 211.21 L 181.62 207.72 Z" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-miterlimit="10" pointer-events="all" style="fill: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255)); stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></path></g></g><g data-cell-id="8fY0ih4KfIxNHs2CR79k-3"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><ellipse cx="419.53" cy="295.14" rx="55.92" ry="16.44" fill="none" stroke="#000000" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></ellipse></g><g><g><switch><foreignObject style="overflow: visible; text-align: left;" pointer-events="none" width="100%" height="100%" requiredFeatures="http://www.w3.org/TR/SVG11/feature#Extensibility"><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="display: flex; align-items: unsafe center; justify-content: unsafe center; width: 110px; height: 1px; padding-top: 295px; margin-left: 365px;"><div style="box-sizing: border-box; font-size: 0; text-align: center; color: #000000; "><div style="display: inline-block; font-size: 12px; font-family: Helvetica; color: light-dark(#000000, #ffffff); line-height: 1.2; pointer-events: all; white-space: normal; word-wrap: normal; ">predicate</div></div></div></foreignObject><text x="420" y="299" fill="#000000" font-family="Helvetica" font-size="12px" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));">predicate</text></switch></g></g></g><g data-cell-id="8fY0ih4KfIxNHs2CR79k-4"><g transform="translate(0.5,0.5)"><ellipse cx="419.53" cy="382.95" rx="55.92" ry="16.44" fill="none" stroke="#000000" pointer-events="all" style="stroke: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));"></ellipse></g><g><g><switch><foreignObject style="overflow: visible; text-align: left;" pointer-events="none" width="100%" height="100%" requiredFeatures="http://www.w3.org/TR/SVG11/feature#Extensibility"><div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" style="display: flex; align-items: unsafe center; justify-content: unsafe center; width: 110px; height: 1px; padding-top: 38